        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "2a341dec-953c-5c37-b7a1-a5cd95c6de7d",
        ":question/problem": "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
